    <div class="page">
        <div class="section-title">INSTRUCTOR'S OVERVIEW OF THE CLASS:</div>
        <p>This course is designed to move beyond the basic concepts and theories used by sociologists to analyze various aspects of social life. We will examine specific social problems with reference to their origins, development, connections to current day events, and suggested solutions.</p>

        <div class="section-title">Course Goals and Learning Outcomes</div>
        <ol>
            <li>The student will demonstrate an understanding of the foundations of contemporary social problems.</li>
            <li>The student will demonstrate an understanding of social problems related to various social institutions.</li>
            <li>The student will demonstrate an understanding of how social inequality contributes to the development of various social problems.</li>
            <li>The student will demonstrate an understanding of the broad implications of social problems related to health and well-being.</li>
            <li>The student will demonstrate an understanding of the emergence of social problems relative to social change.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="page">
        <div class="section-title">CLASS REQUIREMENTS &amp; POLICIES:</div>
        <p>ADA Statement on Discrimination and Disability Support Services:</p>
        <p>If you need classroom accommodations, please contact The Center for Accommodations and Support Services (CASS) at <span data-field="cass.phone">{{ cass.phone }}</span>, or by email to <span data-field="cass.email">{{ cass.email }}</span>, or visit their office located in UTEP Union East, Room 106. For additional information, please visit the CASS website at <span data-field="cass.website">{{ cass.website }}</span>. CASS staff are the only individuals who can validate and, if need be, authorize accommodations for students with disabilities.</p>

        <div class="section-title">ATTENDANCE:</div>
        <p>You should make an effort to ATTEND EVERY CLASS. Arriving prepared and participating in discussions and group or individual work will contribute to success in this class. This is the major element of your participation grade. Please arrive on time and remain until I dismiss you. It is up to you to be in class when work is assigned and collected. Any assignments due at the beginning of class must be submitted by 1:30pm, and must be submitted by you. There is no "grace period". If you arrive late (after 1:30pm) it is highly unlikely your work will be accepted. Students who submit work then leave before class ends will be considered absent and will not receive credit for the assignment.</p>
        <p>All absences are considered unexcused; there is no need for you to provide evidence to explain your absence. Except where UTEP policy dictates otherwise, make-ups will not be allowed for any work missed during your absence.</p>

        <div class="section-title">CLASSROOM ETIQUETTE:</div>
        <p>Please refer to the UTEP Handbook of Operating Procedures for information about conduct, appeals, student's rights and responsibilities.</p>
        <p>You are advised that materials to be discussed in class may be, to some, offensive or controversial. I expect that we will conduct ourselves as adults and with respect for differences of thought and opinion and with respect for ourselves and peers. I reserve the right to dismiss students who are disrupting class in any form. Eating, talking out of turn or to your neighbor instead of adding to the discussion, packing to leave before you are dismissed and sending/reading messages on phones or other devices are some examples of disruptive behavior that may result in you being asked to leave. All electronic devices should be turned off or placed on silent and kept out of sight during class. I reserve the right to answer your phone should it ring during class. Continued instances of any disruptive, disorderly or disrespectful behavior may result in further action including the student being dropped from the course.</p>
        <p>Audio/video recordings are not allowed.</p>
        <p>Academic dishonesty in any form will neither be tolerated or ignored and will be referred to the Dean of Students for disciplinary action.</p>
    </div>

